description:
After the bd:JMAP-3t94.17 fix, MockBackend::create_object at lib.rs:411-442 sets the server id via a defensive pattern match:

```rust
let mut val = serde_json::to_value(&obj)
    .map_err(|e| BackendSetError::Other(MockError(format!("serialize: {e}"))))?;
if let serde_json::Value::Object(ref mut m) = val {
    m.insert(
        "id".to_owned(),
        serde_json::Value::String(server_id.as_ref().to_owned()),
    );
}
```

The canonical MemoryBackend::create_object in the SAME crate (memory.rs:619-621) uses the direct IndexMut form:

```rust
let mut val = serde_json::to_value(&obj)
    .map_err(|e| BackendSetError::Other(MemoryError(format!("serialize: {e}"))))?;
val["id"] = serde_json::Value::String(server_id.as_ref().to_owned());
```

The behavioural difference is semantically meaningful, not stylistic. `serde_json::Value::index_mut` panics if val is not an object; `if let Value::Object(ref mut m) = val` silently skips the id insertion. For an O whose Serialize impl produces a non-object (a hypothetical newtype-serialized-as-string, or a hand-rolled Serialize), the MockBackend form would silently VIOLATE the SharingBackend::create_object id-on-O invariant the bd:JMAP-3t94.17 fix just locked down, while the MemoryBackend form would panic loudly and surface the contract break.

For Principal and ShareNotification (the only types this backend handles in current tests) the difference never fires, because both serialize as objects via #[derive(Serialize)]. But the defensive form weakens the invariant check that the .17 regression test exists to defend — a future SetObject impl that breaks the contract would slip through MockBackend's defensive shape.

Also a cookie-cutter divergence within a single crate: two reference impls that should be teaching the same idiom present two different shapes for the same operation. The canonical form should be adopted in both places.

Recommendation: change lib.rs:432-437 to match memory.rs:619-621 — use `val["id"] = serde_json::Value::String(server_id.as_ref().to_owned());` directly. The .ref mut destructure becomes unnecessary.
